#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267 :topic/difficulty 0.6911657375018757 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428062 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1047->148. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "3afe28a0-5051-52a1-a085-a06727c301b8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096456 :question/id #uuid "201a713a-83ca-5e31-9a4f-257795d7eb1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4690" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=3 \\\\ y+z &=2 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(2)=2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 2-2 = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $z=2$ and $y=0$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(0)+(2)=3\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = 3-2 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x = 1,$ $y=0,$ and $z = 2.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 1,0,2 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8f8937b-de15-5d4c-aa77-1d45595fc7af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096457 :question/id #uuid "cc8ce97a-3bda-506f-b9d2-98ac7524583a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4691" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $x_3=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(5)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-5=-7.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 5$ and $x_2 = -7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx_1+(-7)-(5) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = 1 + 7 + 5 = 13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 13,$ $x_2=-7,$ and $x_3 = 5.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 13,-7,5 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de05090a-2461-514a-971d-b83c7cea124e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096458 :question/id #uuid "7b44089c-8ca7-57e5-b390-7900647e3884" :question/math-academy-id "e-4692" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n3x_3=3 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\tx_3=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(1)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-1=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 1$ and $x_2 = -3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x_1+(-3)-(1) = 0\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (3 + 1) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 2,$ $x_2=-3,$ and $x_3 = 1.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 2,-3,1 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e51c9a-6bce-5ea6-a9fe-febb09f8754d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Given an Augmented Matrix Containing Non-Unit Leading Entries"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1047 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049445}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Back Substitution"}]}
